---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: "Can I add up the amounts after converting?", a: "Yes. Amounts like 2,350.75, (10,000.00) or 15% are saved as real numbers with the same look, so =SUM(), sorting and filters work immediately. Dates and account numbers stay as text so they aren’t changed by Excel." },
  { q: "My bank statement PDF asks for a password. What now?", a: "Banks often lock statements with a password such as your date of birth or CNIC digits. Remove it first with Unlock PDF using that password, then convert the unlocked copy." },
  { q: "Does it work on a phone?", a: "Yes. It runs in Safari on iPhone and Chrome on Android. The .xlsx file goes to Files or Downloads and opens in the Excel or Google Sheets app." },
  { q: "What if my PDF is a scan?", a: "PDFSherpa notices when a PDF has no text and offers OCR in English, Urdu or Hindi. The recognised words are then arranged into rows and columns. Always double-check numbers from a scan." },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="convert-pdf-to-excel-free" faqs={faqs}>
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/pdf-to-excel">PDF to Excel</a>, select your PDF and click <strong>Convert to Excel</strong>. Tables come out as rows and columns, and amounts become numbers you can total. It’s free, there’s no signup or watermark, and the PDF never leaves your device.</p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/pdf-to-excel">pdfsherpa.com/pdf-to-excel</a> on your computer or phone.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Select PDF</strong>, or drop the file on the box.</li>
    <li>Keep <strong>Turn amounts into numbers</strong> ticked if you want to calculate with them.</li>
    <li>Tick <strong>Put each PDF page on its own sheet</strong> only if every page holds a different table. Otherwise all pages go into one long sheet, which is easier to sort and total.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Convert to Excel</strong>, check the preview of the first rows, and download the .xlsx.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>Bank statements</h2>
  <p>
    Statements are the most common reason to convert a PDF to Excel: to total spending by category, match payments or prepare a tax return.
    Each transaction becomes one row with the date, description, debit, credit and balance in separate columns. After converting:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Select the header row and use <em>Data → Filter</em> to sort or filter transactions.</li>
    <li>Type <code>=SUM(C:C)</code> under the debit column to total your spending.</li>
    <li>If the statement spans many pages, the repeated column headers on each page can be removed with the filter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Getting a cleaner table</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Download the original PDF</strong> from your bank or supplier rather than a scan or a photo. A real text PDF gives exact numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Only need some pages?</strong> Keep just those with <a href="/extract-pdf-pages">Extract Pages</a> first, then convert.</li>
    <li><strong>Need just the text?</strong> <a href="/pdf-to-text">PDF to Text</a> copies everything as plain text without columns.</li>
    <li><strong>Mostly paragraphs, not tables?</strong> <a href="/pdf-to-word">PDF to Word</a> gives an editable document instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What isn’t copied</h2>
  <p>
    A PDF only contains the final text and numbers, so formulas can’t be recovered, and charts come across as their labels. Shading, borders, text colours and logos are copied. Columns are
    worked out from where the words sit on each page, so tables with clear gaps between columns convert best; a long note that stretches across the
    table goes into the first column.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>Statements and invoices are private. PDFSherpa reads the PDF and builds the spreadsheet inside your browser: nothing is uploaded, stored or logged.</p>
</GuideLayout>
